    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#ifndef KNN_H\\n#define KNN_H\\n\\n#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float x;\\n    float y;\\n} Point;\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    int n;\\n    float *xs;\\n    float *ys;\\n    char *labels;\\n} Dataset;\\n\\nvoid on_error() {\\n    printf(\\\"Invalid input file.\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(1);\\n}\\n\\nint parse_number_of_groups() {\\n    int n;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" n_groups=%d \\\", &n) != 1) on_error();\\n    return n;\\n}\\n\\nPoint parse_point() {\\n    float x, y;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" (%f ,%f) \\\", &x, &y) != 2) on_error();\\n    Point point;\\n    point.x = x;\\n    point.y = y;\\n    return point;\\n}\\n\\nDataset *parse_dataset() {\\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\\n    Dataset *ds = (Dataset *) malloc(sizeof(Dataset));\\n    if (!ds) on_error();\\n    ds->n = 0;\\n    ds->xs = NULL;\\n    ds->ys = NULL;\\n    ds->labels = NULL;\\n    size_t cap = 0;\\n\\n    for (int g = 0; g < n_groups; ++g) {\\n        char label;\\n        int length;\\n        if (scanf(\\\" label=%c \\\", &label) != 1) on_error();\\n        if (scanf(\\\" length=%d \\\", &length) != 1) on_error();\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {\\n            Point p = parse_point();\\n            if ((size_t)ds->n == cap) {\\n                size_t newcap = cap ? cap * 2 : 1024;\\n                float *nx = (float *) realloc(ds->xs, newcap * sizeof(float));\\n                float *ny = (float *) realloc(ds->ys, newcap * sizeof(float));\\n                char *nl = (char *) realloc(ds->labels, newcap * sizeof(char));\\n                if (!nx || !ny || !nl) on_error();\\n                ds->xs = nx;\\n                ds->ys = ny;\\n                ds->labels = nl;\\n                cap = newcap;\\n            }\\n            ds->xs[ds->n] = p.x;\\n            ds->ys[ds->n] = p.y;\\n            ds->labels[ds->n] = label;\\n            ++ds->n;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    return ds;\\n}\\n\\nint parse_k() {\\n    int k;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" k=%d \\\", &k) != 1) on_error();\\n    return k;\\n}\\n\\n#endif\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.h\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include \\\"knn.h\\\"\\n#include <omp.h>\\n#include <string.h>\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float d;\\n    int idx;\\n} Candidate;\\n\\nstatic int cmp_candidate(const void *a, const void *b) {\\n    const Candidate *ca = (const Candidate *)a;\\n    const Candidate *cb = (const Candidate *)b;\\n    if (ca->d < cb->d) return -1;\\n    if (ca->d > cb->d) return 1;\\n    if (ca->idx < cb->idx) return -1;\\n    if (ca->idx > cb->idx) return 1;\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\\nchar knn(Dataset *ds, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\\n    long n = ds->n;\\n    float tx = to_evaluate.x;\\n    float ty = to_evaluate.y;\\n\\n    // Compute squared distances in parallel with SIMD\\n    float *dists = (float *) malloc(n * sizeof(float));\\n    if (!dists && n > 0) on_error();\\n\\n    float *restrict xs = ds->xs;\\n    float *restrict ys = ds->ys;\\n    float *restrict dptr = dists;\\n\\n    #pragma omp parallel for simd schedule(static)\\n    for (long i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\\n        float dx = xs[i] - tx;\\n        float dy = ys[i] - ty;\\n        dptr[i] = dx * dx + dy * dy;\\n    }\\n\\n    int nt = omp_get_max_threads();\\n    Candidate **local_tops = (Candidate **) malloc(nt * sizeof(Candidate *));\\n    int *local_sizes = (int *) calloc(nt, sizeof(int));\\n    if (!local_tops || !local_sizes) on_error();\\n\\n    for (int t = 0; t < nt; ++t) {\\n        local_tops[t] = (Candidate *) malloc(k * sizeof(Candidate));\\n        if (!local_tops[t]) on_error();\\n    }\\n\\n    #pragma omp parallel\\n    {\\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\\n        int nthreads = omp_get_num_threads();\\n        long chunk = (n + nthreads - 1) / nthreads;\\n        long start = tid * chunk;\\n        long end = start + chunk;\\n        if (end > n) end = n;\\n\\n        Candidate *top = local_tops[tid];\\n        int size = 0;\\n\\n        for (long i = start; i < end; ++i) {\\n            float d = dists[i];\\n            // Insert into sorted list by (d, idx)\\n            int p = 0;\\n            while (p < size && (top[p].d < d || (top[p].d == d && top[p].idx < i))) {\\n                ++p;\\n            }\\n            if (p < size || size < k) {\\n                int max_idx = (size < k ? size : k - 1);\\n                for (int q = max_idx; q > p; --q) {\\n                    top[q] = top[q - 1];\\n                }\\n                top[p].d = d;\\n                top[p].idx = i;\\n                if (size < k) ++size;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        local_sizes[tid] = size;\\n    }\\n\\n    long total_cand = 0;\\n    for (int t = 0; t < nt; ++t) {\\n        total_cand += local_sizes[t];\\n    }\\n\\n    Candidate *all = (Candidate *) malloc(total_cand * sizeof(Candidate));\\n    if (!all && total_cand > 0) on_error();\\n\\n    long pos = 0;\\n    for (int t = 0; t < nt; ++t) {\\n        memcpy(all + pos, local_tops[t], local_sizes[t] * sizeof(Candidate));\\n        pos += local_sizes[t];\\n    }\\n\\n    qsort(all, total_cand, sizeof(Candidate), cmp_candidate);\\n\\n    int m = (k < total_cand ? k : (int)total_cand);\\n    char *labels = (char *) malloc(k * sizeof(char));\\n    if (!labels && k > 0) on_error();\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) labels[i] = -1;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\\n        labels[i] = ds->labels[all[i].idx];\\n    }\\n\\n    int counts[256] = {0};\\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\\n        unsigned char c = (unsigned char) labels[i];\\n        ++counts[c];\\n    }\\n\\n    char best_label = 0;\\n    int best_count = -1;\\n    for (int c = 0; c < 256; ++c) {\\n        if (counts[c] > best_count) {\\n            best_count = counts[c];\\n            best_label = (char)c;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Free temporary resources\\n    free(dists);\\n    for (int t = 0; t < nt; ++t) free(local_tops[t]);\\n    free(local_tops);\\n    free(local_sizes);\\n    free(all);\\n    free(labels);\\n\\n    return best_label;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    Dataset *ds = parse_dataset();\\n    int k = parse_k();\\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%c\\\", knn(ds, k, to_evaluate));\\n\\n    free(ds->xs);\\n    free(ds->ys);\\n    free(ds->labels);\\n    free(ds);\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.c\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Combining flattened contiguous storage (A), OpenMP SIMD parallel distance computation with restrict pointers (B), and deterministic per-thread top-k selection merged by (distance, index) with bucket-count voting (C) should reduce runtime while preserving exact reference semantics including sentinel handling when fewer than k points exist.\",\n  \"notes\": \"Fixed the previous build failure by removing the invalid aligned clause on ds->xs/ds->ys and avoiding posix_memalign implicit declaration; used plain malloc for dists. The implementation flattens input directly into a Dataset during parsing (A), computes squared distances with #pragma omp parallel for simd (B), then performs per-thread top-k selection over static chunks and merges deterministically by (distance, original index) before frequency counting (C). Sentinel -1 labels are padded when n < k to match the reference behavior.\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [\n    \"c2445fbe50614c7a\",\n    \"4f323db2533041fa\",\n    \"8154d1459c9244b3\"\n  ],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Flattened group points into contiguous xs/ys/labels arrays via Dataset struct and direct parsing (A)\",\n    \"Parallel SIMD distance computation with restrict pointers over the flattened index space (B)\",\n    \"Per-thread top-k selection merged deterministically by (distance, original index) and bucket-count frequency voting (C)\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one candidate integrating the three source ideas. We have current baseline code in knn.c and knn.h. The integration sources are A (c2445fbe50614c7a), B (4f323db2533041fa), C (8154d1459c9244b3). We need to produce a single candidate that combines these transformations, likely the best performing one from history was c596fa3c95c845d2 which had mean 0.02615 and score 0.9876. That experiment used sources b29cec62b6a945b9 (not in our list), 4f323db2533041fa, 8154d1459c9244b3. But we are constrained to only use the three given integration_source_ids: c2445fbe50614c7a, 4f323db2533041fa, 8154d1459c9244b3. So we must integrate those three.\n\nWe need to produce a single candidate that combines flattened storage (A), OpenMP SIMD distance computation with restrict and aligned allocations (B), and per-thread top-k selection with deterministic merge (C). However, the previous attempt 6d8be36ec5184163 combined A+B+C but failed build due to pragma syntax error: `#pragma omp parallel for simd schedule(static) aligned(ds->xs, ds->ys, dists: 64)` - that's invalid because aligned clause expects variable names not expressions. Also posix_memalign implicit declaration warning (need _GNU_SOURCE or use malloc). We need to fix those.\n\nWe should produce a correct integrated version. Let's design carefully.\n\nThe current baseline uses Group* groups and knn(int n_groups, Group *groups, int k, Point to_evaluate). The A transformation changed the API to Dataset* ds with xs, ys, labels arrays. But we must preserve the input/output contract: main() parses using existing parse functions? Actually A's diff shows it changed parsing to append points directly into flat arrays and rewrote knn(Dataset *ds,...). However, our current source is baseline (original) not A. We need to integrate all three transformations onto the baseline. The integration sources are descriptions of what each did; we can choose which parts to adopt.\n\nWe must ensure correctness: exact output bytes against reference. The original algorithm uses euclidean_distance_no_sqrt and tie-breaking by insertion order? Actually original code inserts into sorted distances array with condition `d < distances[x] || distances[x] == -1`. It does not use index tie-break; it just keeps first k smallest, but if equal distance, the later point will be inserted only if d < existing (strict), so earlier points win ties. Then qsort labels and picks most frequent with tie-breaking by order after sort? Actually qsort on char array sorts ascending; then frequency counting: when current_frequency > most_frequent_count, update; else not. So for equal frequencies, the first encountered label in sorted order wins (since only strictly greater updates). That is deterministic.\n\nThe C transformation uses per-thread top-k with tie-breaking by (distance, index) to preserve exact semantics? Actually it sorts candidates by distance then index ascending, so among equal distances lower original index comes first. Then picks k smallest; that matches original insertion order because earlier points have smaller indices and are inserted before later ones if equal distance? Let's check: original loop iterates groups in order, points within group in order. For each point, it inserts into sorted list only if d < existing or slot empty. So for equal distances, the first encountered (smaller index) is kept; later equal-distance points are not inserted because condition `d < distances[x]` false when equal. So top-k selection with tie-break by smaller index yields same set of k nearest neighbors as original? Actually if there are more than k points with distance less than some threshold, the first k encountered (by iteration order) among those with smallest distances will be selected; but if multiple have same distance at boundary, only earlier ones get in. So using (distance, index) ascending and taking first k gives exactly that: sort by distance then index, take first k. Yes.\n\nSo C's approach is correct for top-k selection. Then frequency counting: original qsort labels then counts; tie-breaking among equal frequencies picks the label that appears earliest in sorted order of labels array (which after qsort is ascending char). That means if two labels have same max count, the smaller character value wins because when scanning sorted array, first time current_frequency > most_frequent_count updates only on strictly greater. So we need to replicate that tie-breaking.\n\nC's frequency counting uses counts[256] and then picks best with `if (counts[c] > best_count)` so it iterates c from 0 upward; thus for equal counts, smaller char wins because later ones not update if equal. That matches original? Original after qsort labels ascending, scanning left to right: when a new label appears, current_frequency resets; at end of run, if current_frequency > most_frequent_count then update. So the first label (smallest) that achieves max count wins. Since counts array iterates c increasing, same behavior. Good.\n\nSo we can adopt C's frequency counting.\n\nNow B adds OpenMP SIMD distance computation and restrict pointers, aligned allocations. A flattens storage into Dataset struct with xs, ys, labels arrays. We need to combine: parse input directly into flat arrays (A), then compute distances in parallel using OpenMP SIMD over flattened indices (B), then per-thread top-k selection (C) or maybe a single heap? The previous successful experiment c596fa3c95c845d2 used sources b29cec62b6a945b9, 4f323db2533041fa, 8154d1459c9244b3. That likely combined B and C but not A (since b29... is unknown). It achieved best time ~0.026s. Our integration must include A as well. But we can still use flattened arrays from A.\n\nWe need to produce a single candidate that compiles and runs correctly on ARM64 with 4 cores, OpenMP available? The runtime says OMP_NUM_THREADS=4, architecture ARM64. We should assume GCC/OpenMP works.\n\nLet's design the integrated code:\n\n- Keep knn.h with parsi